    <section class="mt-8" aria-labelledby="principles-heading"><h2 id="principles-heading" class="section-title">Editorial and source-verification principles</h2><ul class="mt-2 list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-sm text-brand-body">
        <li><span class="font-medium text-brand-navy">Primary sources first.</span> Every record links to an official document or page. Secondary sources are used only when clearly labelled and never as the sole basis for a legal claim.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-brand-navy">No invention.</span> If a date, penalty, obligation or outcome cannot be established from the source, the field is left empty and the confidence level is set to "unavailable". We never fill gaps with assumptions.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-brand-navy">Original summaries.</span> Summaries are written in plain language with article or section references. We do not reproduce copyrighted legal commentary, paid database content or standards text such as ISO/IEC documents.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-brand-navy">Visible uncertainty.</span> Proposed laws, differing application dates, pending amendments and unconfirmed facts are labelled as such in the record and on the page.</li>
        <li><span class="font-medium text-brand-navy">Human verification.</span> A record is marked verified only when a named reviewer has opened the official source and confirmed each field. Until then it shows "human verification pending".</li>
